  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 142 AM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nIt sure looks and feels more like fall than the middle of July\nearly this morning with deep upper-level cyclonic flow anchored\nby seasonably cool temperatures and pockets of lake-induced\nstratocumulus clouds! The change in regime is exemplified by a\ndrop in 800mb temperatures of about 12C/24 hours per the last\ntwo 00Z RAOBs at GRB, a range from roughly the 90th to 10th \npercentiles. The stage is thus set for a beautiful stretch of \ndays with seasonably cool temperatures, low humidity values, and\nno threat for rain. Highs today will top off in the low to mid \n70s and increase by a few degrees each day through the end of \nthe week. Overnight lows will be comfortable and start in the \nlower 50s tonight rising to the lower 60s by Friday night. The \nonly fly in the ointment in an otherwise pleasant period is \nhazardous waves along all southern Lake Michigan beaches today \nthrough Thursday morning.\n\nThe reprieve from the heat will be short-lived as upper-level\nridging redevelops across the central United States this\nweekend. Highs will climb back into the upper 80s to lower 90s\nas early as Sunday and continue through next week. Humidity\nlevels are also poised to rise with agricultural\nevapotranspiration likely augmenting near-surface dew points \ninto the mid 70s. The next opportunity for storms will arrive in\nthe Sunday/Monday (July 26/27) timeframe along the precarious \nnortheastern side of the building upper-level ridge. The EPS in\nparticular is bullish with 51-member mean MUCAPE already \nexceeding 2000 J/kg and over 50% of members with a PWAT \nexceeding 2 inches. This will be a period to watch for severe \nweather in the general Upper Mississippi River Valley/Great \nLakes region.\n\nBorchardt",
